Hello all,

I'm sorry if this question has been answered, but I can't
seem to find an explanation. I am building a n-mixture removal model with both
site and observation covariates. We are interested in incorporating day of year (doy) into the detection model as a proxy to leafout conditions as the surveys took place from spring to early summer. We expect to see a decrease in detection as doy increases. I understand the importance of standardizing
continuous variables, but I am unsure how to approach it with observation
covariates specifically. For my example, I'll include a portion of the first
line of data, but there are 57 sites and up to 8 survey events.

Shown below is an example of doy values for one site

              1
        2        3
    4         5
 6

1         122     124
    131     133     137
...

2          ...

I have seen 2 different methods used in various tutorials
and examples.

scale(data[,c(1:6)]) which standardizes by survey event
(each column).

            1
                2
                3
                4
               5

-0.71665004 -1.77807859 -1.25370502 -1.351981676 -1.25656001

And the standardize function from wiqid package.

standardize(as.matrix(data[,c(1:6)]))

This calculates a single mean and standard deviation for all
the survey events and applies it to each column. Which gives results like this

           1
               2
             3
              4
         5

-1.6601355 -1.4703218 -0.80597377 -0.61616004 -0.2365326

The method I use definitely changes my results, so I want to
