Hi everyone! I apologize if this question has been answered elsewhere. I read the Mackenzie and Bailey 2004 paper and  I've dug around the google group all week trying to find the answer but I'm still deep in the weeds here.

I'm running single season occupancy models for my thesis based on camera data I collected at 35 sites over a period of 31 weeks. My thesis committee seems happy with the models, but I still need to assess the goodness of fit. I ran a parametric bootstrap in unmarked but I'm struggling to interpret the results. Here are the results for one of the models:

Parametric Bootstrap Statistics:
              t0 mean(t0 - t_B) StdDev(t0 - t_B) Pr(t_B > t0)
SSE          157          0.534             11.9        0.498
Chisq        702        -37.282             73.2        0.758
freemanTukey 211          2.836             14.2        0.440

t_B quantiles:
              0% 2.5% 25% 50% 75% 97.5% 100%
SSE          107  132 149 157 165   179  190
Chisq        493  571 705 742 782   882 1169
freemanTukey 150  180 200 209 218   235  245

The p-values are higher than 0.05 which seems good in this context, but I'm not quite sure what the rest means. I also wasn't sure if this was the right method to use. I saw some other posts where it was recommended to use the chi-square test in AICcmodavg which sorts the data into cohorts based on different combinations of possible detection histories, but almost all of my sites have different detection histories, so only one cohort has more than one site.

Any advice would be appreciated!

Best,
Matt

